      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The grocer's display follows a pattern where the top row has 1 can, the second row has 1 + 2 = 3 cans, the third row has 3 + 2 = 5 cans, and so on. We can see that the number of cans in each row forms an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a_1 = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\).\n\nLet \\(n\\) be the number of rows. The total number of cans in the display is the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of this arithmetic sequence. The sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by the formula:\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\left(2a_1 + (n-1)d\\right)\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a_1 = 1\\) and \\(d = 2\\) into the formula, we get:\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\left(2 \\cdot 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2\\right) = \\frac{n}{2} \\left(2 + 2n - 2\\right) = \\frac{n}{2} \\left(2n\\right) = n^2\n\\]\nWe know that the total number of cans is 100, so we set up the equation:\n\\[\nn^2 = 100\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nn = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\nTherefore, the display contains \\(\\boxed{10}\\) rows.",
